WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.620
Hello, and welcome
to "Cloud OnAir,"

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.810
our live webinars
from Google Cloud that

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.040
happen every Tuesday.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.130
Today we're talking
about the future

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:16.300
of databases in the cloud.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.970
And my name is Deepti
Srivastava I am the product

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.370
manager for Cloud Spanner.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.900
So let's get started.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:29.170
So before we talk
about Spanner and why

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.320
we think it's the future
of databases in cloud,

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.800
I think it's important to go
through a little bit of how

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:43.120
we got here, all of us-- how
IT and databases and just data

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.210
itself got here.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.240
For cloud, it is still
very, very early.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.990
So most of global IT spend,
as you can see from this chart

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:56.350
that we've got
from Gartner here,

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.700
from last year's "Gartner
Reports", basically very little

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.070
of it is still in cloud.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:09.160
So each of these businesses and
each of these infrastructures

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.480
have a database, right?

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.610
You always need
to have a database

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.700
to store your critical data
or not so critical data.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:21.640
So for database spend, it's
extremely early in terms

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.780
of cloud adoption.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:28.360
So as you can see here first
of all, most of database

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:35.260
spend is in operational
DBMSs and very little of it

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.430
is actually in the sort of
non-relational open source

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:40.630
world.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.670
Despite all the
sort of marketing

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.520
you hear from the non-relational
side of the spectrum here,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.860
they're actually--
most data is actually

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.644
stored in relational databases.

00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:52.060
And that's sort
of obvious, right,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.200
because with relational
databases you get transactions,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:01.150
you get atomicity of updates,
you get integrity of data,

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.000
you get consistency
guarantees, et cetera,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.090
which are, of course very,
very useful as we all know

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.340
for developing applications.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:20.460
So obviously this implies
that a lot of the databases

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.490
out there are
relational and most

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.730
of the mission critical
data that a company has

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.430
will be stored in
relational databases.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:34.322
So before we jump into the
growth of relational databases,

00:02:34.322 --> 00:02:36.030
it's important to talk
about what we mean

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.020
by mission critical, right?

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.311
So when we're talking about
mission critical data, what

00:02:39.311 --> 00:02:43.990
we really mean is data
that is business critical,

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.260
so loss of that data
or even a glitch

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:52.800
in serving that data
affects your reputation

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.150
and has financial
risk to your company.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:59.100
So all of this data
that is required

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.350
for you to run your
business is what

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.780
we call mission critical data.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.530
And we're saying that
most of that mission

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:09.750
critical data is stored in most
cases in relational databases.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:14.560
So again, where are we at
with the current status

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.750
of databases?

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:20.370
So most companies, as we talked
about earlier, most of the IT

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.330
spend is still on prem.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.850
What that means is that
most databases are still

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:24.720
running on prem.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.060
And most companies
are then managing

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.210
their own inflexible
database infrastructure

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.490
on premises with
an army of people

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.580
trying to maintain
that infrastructure,

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.820
and maintain their databases,
and keep them alive

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:39.510
and running.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.210
And you know, of course,
there is a huge overhead

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.670
in having those.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.580
There are those companies
who have taken the leap

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.280
and adopted cloud
to some extent.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.490
And what that means is
they're using infrastructure

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.730
as a service from
cloud providers

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.860
so they're running
their own database

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.820
solutions on top of these
cloud provided infrastructure.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.330
So they're still in the business
of maintaining, managing,

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.700
and administering, and
keeping their databases alive.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.012
They may not need to run
the machines themselves

00:04:11.012 --> 00:04:12.720
that host those
databases, but they still

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.690
have to deal with the database
maintenance and management.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.959
And then there are companies who
have taken a bigger leap where

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:20.880
they're actually
paying a service

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.310
provider to manage and run their
database solution in the cloud.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:24.810
Right.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.430
So this means that
they don't have

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.640
to worry about actually
keeping the database alive.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.290
There is a service
that's doing that.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.070
But that still
means that they have

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:39.270
to make sure that the databases
are running the way that's

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.030
expected and they have
to deal with the service

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.980
provider for their
database service

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.820
and maybe deal with other
providers for other services.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:47.820
Right.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.830
So there is some
complication there.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:54.780
Now apart from where the systems
and databases are running,

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.440
the other limitation is
in technology itself.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.140
So companies are running
into limitations around RDBMS

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.720
scale up and scale out
and sharding solutions.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.864
So if you run out of
capacity of your database,

00:05:05.864 --> 00:05:06.780
what do you do, right?

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.120
You have to either scale
up to bigger machines

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.795
or you have to go to
the shard solutions

00:05:10.795 --> 00:05:12.420
and they come with
their own headaches,

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.150
which we'll get into
in a little bit.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:20.030
And finally, companies are
dealing with the administration

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:24.150
and consistency challenges
of scalable solutions

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.390
that are also NoSQL
or non-relational.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.430
So for those
companies who already

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:34.560
hit the problem with their
databases and the scale of them

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.660
and have adopted sort
of more stable solutions

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.170
that are NoSQL or
non-relational,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.670
they're trading off
consistency for scale

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:46.270
or the traditional relational
semantics for scale.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.560
They're running into challenges
of having the consistency

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.060
issues and the
challenges in running

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.880
and in administering these
NoSQL solutions whether on prem

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:54.770
or in the cloud.

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:58.100
But the technology
challenges still remain.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:04.080
So you know what does that
mean for where we are in terms

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.620
of the spectrum of technology?

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:11.250
So we have this very
interesting trade

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.930
between traditional
relational databases

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.430
and traditional
non-relational databases.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:15.000
Right.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.040
So on the business side,
traditional databases

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.720
provide you with schema,
with SQL, with consistency.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.270
And the NoSQL or
nontraditional solutions

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.400
don't provide you those,
but they trade those off

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.790
for high availability,
horizontal scalability,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:34.080
replication, et cetera, which
is the exact limitations

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.020
of traditional relational
databases in most cases.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:44.370
What that means is when you
want to run high availability

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:48.510
relational databases for your
mission critical applications,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:53.250
you have to create these
highly complex architectures

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:57.540
to provide that availability,
and replication, and disaster

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.750
recovery solutions for
your mission critical data.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:05.310
And as you can see, you're
running multiple masters.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.860
You're running read
replicas for scaling out

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:13.320
and offloading the read-only
data access, et cetera.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.870
You're trying to have Memcache
in there somewhere so that you

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.130
can do faster reads
of your data, which

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:23.730
means you have to deal with
the flushing of the cache.

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:26.010
You have to deal with
consistency issues

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.440
with having multiple
masters if you need that.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.520
You have backup replicas.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.420
So managing this
system, now you've

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.240
gone from one database
that hosts your mission

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.620
critical data to this
complicated architecture that,

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.510
again, requires an
army to maintain.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.400
You don't actually know
where the bottlenecks

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.160
are in some cases, because
the system is so fragile.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.579
And you know, God forbid if
one piece of it somehow fails

00:07:51.579 --> 00:07:54.120
and then you have to figure out
what the cascade effects are.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.710
And in most cases,
you don't actually

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.990
even know what the
full picture is

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.120
because it's become such a
complicated, organically grown

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:01.620
beast.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:09.120
So going back to
first principles,

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.650
like what do customers want?

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.510
What do you want
from your database?

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:13.080
Right.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.580
So obviously you want it to be
highly available and scalable.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.500
And you want it to give
you data consistency

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.830
so you can access
data confidently

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.700
for your application and you can
serve the correct and accurate

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.740
data to your customers.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.560
And of course, from a
developer standpoint,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.330
when you're building and
using these databases,

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:35.669
you want simplified operations.

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:36.169
Right?

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:37.770
You want standards.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.864
You want single points
of contact for support.

00:08:40.864 --> 00:08:42.780
You don't want to have
to talk to the database

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:47.100
provider, and the infrastructure
manager, and the cloud provider

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.030
separately.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:51.570
You want to have one
person or one contact point

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:54.430
that you can go to if
you have any issues.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.480
So you don't have
to deal with crises

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.870
by like being the coordinator of
various pieces in that crisis,

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:02.130
right?

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.110
Basically, you
want your databases

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.420
to run while you
can build and grow

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.920
your application
and your business.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:12.000
And this is where
Spanner comes in.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.620
So why did we build Spanner?

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.840
And how did we get here?

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.400
So if we go back
in time to 2005,

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.770
technical
infrastructure at Google

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:29.370
had various scalable solutions,
because we had already

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:31.960
reached the skill versus
consistency problem.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:32.460
Right.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.840
So we had scalable
data storage systems,

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:41.100
but we were lacking
in transactions

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.830
and relational semantics
in those systems.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.040
And as we've learned,
as we all know,

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.970
those relational semantics, SQL,
et cetera, are very important

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:55.410
and they really help you
run your business well.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.110
So Google reached that point.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:01.630
And so the need of the
hour was horizontally

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.830
scaling databases,
brief ACID transactions,

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.030
and global consistency.

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.240
And of course, we are Google,
so we want no downtime.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:10.616
We want high availability.

00:10:10.616 --> 00:10:12.240
We want you to be
able to access Google

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.020
from anywhere at all times.

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:15.870
So we came up with Spanner.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.240
We developed Spanner
with a lot of research

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.850
and a lot of
understanding of how

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:26.370
to build distributed, scalable,
highly available systems.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:28.440
And what we have here
is basically the paper

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.080
that we published in 2012 that
talks about the technology

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.910
that we built at the time.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:37.860
So Spanner was
launched externally

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:42.330
as Cloud Spanner, which is
a publicly available product

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.790
on Google Cloud platform.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:46.840
And it's about
exactly a year ago

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.050
that it was launched
publicly on GCP.

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:53.090
So we're celebrating
our first anniversary

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.040
as a cloud database.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:00.780
So Cloud Spanner is basically
an enterprise grade globally

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:03.750
distributed and strongly
consistent database service

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:06.300
built specifically for
the cloud to combine

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:10.950
the benefits of relational
database structure

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:14.110
with non-relational
horizontal scale.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.189
So what does that mean?

00:11:16.189 --> 00:11:17.730
We're basically
saying you don't have

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:22.080
to trade off or make compromises
between relational semantics

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:26.220
and horizontal scale, which is
what traditionally companies

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:32.490
and IT folks have had to do,
database users have had to do.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:35.280
They've had to basically
trade off and give up

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.820
on relational semantics and
build that in the application

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.480
to get horizontal scale
to manage their data

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.630
growth over the
course of the data

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:49.500
exploding it for
their business, right?

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.980
So Spanner allows you to not
have to make that compromise.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:57.480
It's our no compromise
relational database service.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.550
And you get all of this packaged
up in a managed service.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:02.190
So you can focus
on your database

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.450
and not have to
worry about running--

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:04.950
sorry.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:07.116
You can focus on the
application using that database

00:12:07.116 --> 00:12:09.790
and not have to worry about
running the database itself.

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:14.250
So we provide schemas,
asset transactions, SQL,

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:19.080
and the relational scale with
up to five lines of availability

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:22.200
and an availability SLA,
so we guarantee that in

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:26.080
a fully managed, easily
scalable service.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.640
So if we hark back
to our chart of what

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:34.350
are the pros and
cons of relational

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.560
versus traditional
non-relational services

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:40.372
or databases,
basically you don't

00:12:40.372 --> 00:12:41.580
have to make those tradeoffs.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.680
We have schemas, we have
SQL, we have consistency

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:45.600
like traditional
relational databases,

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.240
and we have availability,
scale, and replication.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.140
And by the way, a bunch
of it out of the box

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:56.460
that you can get with
nontraditional non-relational

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:59.730
traditional NoSQL systems.

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:02.610
So again, you don't
have to make compromises

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:06.360
between technical needs
and business needs

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.820
in order to really
run your applications

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:10.350
and your business critical data.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:18.030
Now there are differences
in the technology of Spanner

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.530
and traditional
relational databases

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:22.830
and how they've worked so far.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:25.470
And of course there
is innovation here

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:28.311
that allows us to do this
relational with scale.

00:13:28.311 --> 00:13:30.060
And I can touch upon
a little bit of that.

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:31.726
But the important
thing to note here is,

00:13:31.726 --> 00:13:35.250
like traditional databases,
you get tables, you get DDL,

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:39.010
you get the same schemas
that you're used to.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:42.630
So you do create table, you
have primary keys, et cetera.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.390
And in this picture,
you can see that we've

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:49.740
created two different tables,
which have relationships

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:51.420
that you can see.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:54.000
And you use simple
DDL statements

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.530
in order to create this table.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:14:02.190
And you'll use this schema
change, DDL statements as well,

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.960
to alter Table or drop
column, et cetera as well.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:08.820
So again, these are
the familiar constructs

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:11.700
that you're used to so far
with the traditional relational

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:16.990
that you get with Spanner as
well with some added benefits.

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:17.490
Right.

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:21.010
So in this case, you
see these two tables.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:26.930
And if you were to see how this
logical structure is laid out,

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:28.620
Spanner has a concept
of interleaving.

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:30.450
It's one of the many
technical differences,

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:31.830
but it's interesting
to point out

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.230
as it kind of
showcases how we've

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:38.130
been built for scale with
the relational transactional

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.462
semantics.

00:14:39.462 --> 00:14:41.670
So in the previous case, we
had two different tables.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.003
And they're logically different.

00:14:43.003 --> 00:14:45.520
There's the albums table
and the singers table.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.690
But if you interleave the albums
table and the singers table,

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:52.950
what that means is that the
data is logically co-located.

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:55.770
So you essentially can
think of it as a pre-join.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:00.810
So when you're doing a large
data set reads or scans,

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:05.880
you don't have to actually
do the join at a run time.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:06.380
Right.

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:09.240
You can predetermine
that these datasets are

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:13.740
going to be queried together
and you can logically

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.920
tell the system to store
them together so that they

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.210
can be retrieved much faster.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:23.340
Now for a few megs or
a few gigs of data,

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:28.030
joining may not be a
latency causing issue,

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:30.984
but when you have hundreds
of gigabytes or hundreds

00:15:30.984 --> 00:15:32.400
of terabytes of
data, and you need

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:37.440
to do all albums for all
singers sort of a query,

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:40.140
that's a lot of data to
be joined and processed

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:42.640
at runtime, at query time.

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:45.420
So instead of having to
do a join at that time,

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.220
you can specify to
the system that you

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:49.740
need to pre-join the
dataset because your most

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:53.610
common queries are actually
going to scan the singers along

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:54.870
with their albums.

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:59.790
So in this case, the
system can very quickly

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:02.220
basically read the data and
return the query results

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:05.250
without having to do the
computationally intensive join,

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:12.660
hence reducing read time
or query serving time.

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:17.100
Again, you can use interleaving
for indexes as well.

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:18.540
So we have secondary indexes.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:19.800
We are a relational database.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.890
And from that standpoint,
you can, of course,

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:27.240
have separate logical
indexes, which

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:31.470
can be standard tables in
other traditional databases.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:32.100
Right.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.200
And in our case, they are
standard tables as well,

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:35.910
but you can interleave them.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:39.000
So again, if you're going
to have to back join indexes

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:43.530
with the base table because your
most common query is requiring

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:46.650
that pattern, then you
can interleave the indexes

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:49.710
within the base table.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:53.400
And again, that can lead to
much faster selects and queries

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:55.000
of your data.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.740
So this is just one of the ways
in which Spanner is different,

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:01.500
but it shows you
under the covers

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.460
the technological
differences we have

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:09.119
which allows us to provide
scale with relational semantics.

00:17:09.119 --> 00:17:16.655
So as Nick says in this
quote from Gartner,

00:17:16.655 --> 00:17:18.544
so when we talked
about-- when we launched

00:17:18.544 --> 00:17:20.710
Spanner and we talked about
it with various analysts

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:25.369
and of course we have
customers that are using it,

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:27.670
it's interesting
because it's not just

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:29.560
another take on a
traditional database.

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:32.060
It's really a new
way of thinking,

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:36.700
a new need that's being met by
the technology that's Spanner.

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:38.920
But Spanner is not
just software, right?

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:45.100
It's the combination of a lot
of very clever software, which

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:48.010
there is a lot of, along
with hardware that we've

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:55.030
built specifically for scale and
to provide the kinds of things

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:58.030
that we need for large, highly
available, scalable system

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:01.330
such as atomic clocks
that we have in-house.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:03.160
Like true time is
a technology we

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:07.390
use for global synchronization
and external consistency, which

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:10.960
is stronger than
linearizability.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:15.070
So we use true time, which is
based on atomic clocks and GPS,

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:17.050
to do this coordination.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:20.470
And that powers
Cloud Spanner as well

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:23.300
and allows it to have that
global external consistency,

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:25.960
which allows us to
then have globally

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:30.940
consistent and strong
relational semantics

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:34.510
database in Cloud Spanner.

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:37.060
This is, of course, build on
top of our Google network,

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:40.140
which we've talked
about quite a bunch,

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.430
and there is a lot
of white papers

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.470
that we've actually put
online as well that one

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:46.540
can read if interested.

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:48.280
But basically,
it's a combination

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:53.290
of our Google networking,
which is highly redundant

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:56.500
and optimized on all levels
from the network fabric

00:18:56.500 --> 00:19:00.310
all the way to the
switches to the hubs,

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:04.450
et cetera, and atomic clocks
and GPS with true time

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:07.960
as well as clever software
in Cloud Spanner itself.

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.990
So we, for example, have
wrote our own implementation

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:11.980
of Paxos.

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:14.480
We don't use open source
Paxos implementation.

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:18.820
We optimized the Paxos
algorithm in our implementation

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:21.910
for our specific use
case, which allows

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:24.490
us to again do low
latency transactions

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:27.400
and to do strong reads
across continents

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:30.190
and across oceanic links.

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:34.140
And all of this combines
to give you Cloud Spanner.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:41.950
So there's a bunch of customers
that are using Spanner

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:43.980
and they're excited about it.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:47.440
I am personally excited to
talk about a few of them

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:50.200
that I've been seeing them win.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:53.740
And it's really
exciting for us to see

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:57.710
how the world has adopted
and started to use Spanner.

00:19:57.710 --> 00:20:03.040
So It's not just about
trading off cap x for op

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:05.320
x, which is what a managed
database service gives you.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:06.910
That's kind of the
promise of cloud.

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:08.800
But with cloud,
customers are trying

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:11.881
to do inherently new
and exciting things

00:20:11.881 --> 00:20:13.630
that they couldn't do
before, because they

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.290
were limited by technology.

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:19.660
And in this case, Optiva, which
is a telecom communications

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:22.900
billing and charging
system, so as mission

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:26.530
critical as they
get, it has basically

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:30.850
adopted Spanner as its next
generation database system

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:36.010
for its next generation software
for billing and charging.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:38.130
Right.

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:40.390
You know, when they
looked at Spanner,

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:43.960
they saw improved
performance and reliability

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:46.510
from their existing
traditional architectures

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:49.545
and they actually saw 10
times more throughput,

00:20:49.545 --> 00:20:51.740
so 10 times more
transactions per second,

00:20:51.740 --> 00:20:53.860
at a dramatically reduced cost.

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:58.450
So Optiva is actually presenting
this week at Mobile World

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:01.510
Congress in Barcelona,
and I'm here with you

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.940
and I'm not there, but
it's really exciting

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:08.170
because you know they're really
excited about the technology.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:10.630
They're really excited
about the possibilities

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:14.140
it opens up for them
and for their customers.

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:18.700
And that's really the reason to
have Cloud Spanner as a managed

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:19.450
database service.

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:22.240
They don't have to worry
about the system itself.

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:23.890
And also because
of its combination

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:27.350
of relational semantics
and horizontal scale,

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:29.530
they can do things
with this technology

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:33.700
that they couldn't do with their
existing on prem solutions.

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:38.350
So another interesting example,
one of my favorites actually,

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:41.080
Marketo and the one
following, is again--

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:45.910
Marketo is a marketing
platform that

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:49.900
provides marketing
applications for companies

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:51.340
all over the world.

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:53.950
And for them, in
their own words,

00:21:53.950 --> 00:21:58.120
having order sequence in
their application logic

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:59.560
is very important.

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:03.200
And as they were
looking to go to cloud,

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:06.670
and looking obviously--
you know the first step

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:09.670
is which cloud provider do I
use and the second question

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:13.510
for most of us is, well,
what database services do

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:17.300
each of these cloud providers
have and how do I use them?

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:19.780
Is it easier for me
to use these services

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:23.060
or is it going to make
my life more difficult.

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:27.310
And with Cloud Spanner,
basically again they

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:32.206
could use the system to have
the kind of order and sequence

00:22:32.206 --> 00:22:33.580
guarantees and
[? the op ?] logic

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.280
so they didn't have to
write brittle application

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:38.350
logic to have that sequencing.

00:22:38.350 --> 00:22:43.720
With NoSQL and with systems
that are scaled but lacking

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:47.590
in transactional consistency or
strong consistency, all of that

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:49.210
has to be built
into the app, which

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:51.790
means your application
becomes a lot more complicated

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:55.330
and your team is
basically stressing

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:57.790
in keeping that system
alive and running

00:22:57.790 --> 00:23:03.940
instead of building cool
things to serve your customers.

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:06.520
That obviously is a
productivity hit, but also

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:07.840
a growth hit for you.

00:23:07.840 --> 00:23:09.689
It affects your
business bottom line.

00:23:09.689 --> 00:23:11.230
And in this case,
when you don't have

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:14.017
to worry about that anymore,
you can choose Spanner.

00:23:14.017 --> 00:23:16.600
You don't have to re-platform
or think of re-platforming ever.

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:19.330
You just grow as your
global business grows.

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.140
I mean that's a powerful
statement, right?

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:28.000
And another one of our
favorite customers, Evernote--

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:30.760
I think a lot of
people know Evernote.

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:33.820
Again, they had a multiple
hundred sharded [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:35.410
SQL system.

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:38.950
They want their users' notes
to be obviously available

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:41.710
no matter where they are.

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:47.470
They, with Cloud Spanner, can
go to a system that reduces this

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:50.560
complexity, gives them scale,
and once they have migrated,

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:54.400
they don't actually have to
think about re-platforming ever

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:57.940
again so it reduces,
in fact eliminates,

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:03.100
all of that stress of managing
the complicated multi shard

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:09.220
MySQL system that keeps
their operations team awake

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:09.940
at night--

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:14.350
from that to building
more functionality

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:17.980
and having an ease of
use of their application

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:21.730
and infrastructure.

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:28.240
So we talked about some of
the positive things in Cloud

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:30.897
Spanner or how it's different
from traditional relational

00:24:30.897 --> 00:24:32.980
non-relation databases,
but it's important to note

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:36.700
that Google Cloud Platform's
database portfolio is vast.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:38.610
and Spanner is one piece of it.

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:39.110
Right.

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:45.430
We actually have Memcache all
the way on the left to our data

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:47.190
warehouse in the
cloud with BigQuery.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:50.890
So the way I think about
Cloud Spanner and BigQuery,

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:56.380
if you're used to traditional
relational databases and data

00:24:56.380 --> 00:24:59.800
warehouses, Cloud
Spanner and BigQuery

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:02.680
are the cloud
equivalents of those.

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:05.591
But it's deliberately
placed next to--

00:25:05.591 --> 00:25:07.090
Cloud Spanner is
deliberately placed

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:09.760
next to non-relational
systems in this chart

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:14.320
because it allows you to
have non-relational scale

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:16.810
along with the relational
semantics that it offers.

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:20.470
So you know the whole
portfolio basically

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:22.030
allows you to have
a bunch of tools

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:25.600
that you can choose from to
build the kinds of systems

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:27.400
you need for your application.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:31.270
We're not trying to tie you down
to one and only one solution,

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:34.032
but we're not giving you
hundreds to choose from.

00:25:34.032 --> 00:25:35.740
We think they're
different use cases that

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:39.250
require very specific tailored
solutions for storage.

00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:41.890
And with this
portfolio, we basically

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:44.820
offer you a solution for each
of those specific use cases.

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:51.340
What that also
means is, again, you

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:55.180
have the toolkit to
choose the right system,

00:25:55.180 --> 00:26:00.040
but you're not left on
your own to figure out

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:04.690
which system you need to use
among hundreds or dozens.

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:08.890
There is, as I said, a solution
for each specific use case.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:13.730
And we hope you choose the
right system for the use case.

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:18.490
So as this chart shows,
there's basically a flow chart

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:20.830
that you can use to
pick the system you

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:23.200
want for the use case you have.

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:27.550
And this is again up on our
docs in a cloud.google.com

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:30.280
so you can look it up
there as well along

00:26:30.280 --> 00:26:33.080
with a lot more information.

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:37.090
So hopefully that
kind of gave you

00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:40.435
an overview of why we
think Cloud Spanner is

00:26:40.435 --> 00:26:42.447
the future of database systems.

00:26:42.447 --> 00:26:44.530
And we'll take a short
break of less than a minute

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:47.457
and come back for a live
Q&amp;A section as well.

00:26:47.457 --> 00:26:48.040
So stay tuned.

00:27:26.380 --> 00:27:28.210
Welcome back to "Cloud OnAir."

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:31.300
Let's get straight to
our audience questions

00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:34.300
for the day from this webinar.

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:39.760
So question 1, are you expecting
all relational databases

00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:41.380
to migrate to cloud immediately?

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:42.510
Absolutely not.

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:48.700
Like there are many reasons
why most of the cloud

00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:49.650
spend is still--

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:52.420
sorry, most of the IT spend
is still on prem and not

00:27:52.420 --> 00:27:55.580
in the cloud as I had
mentioned earlier in my talk.

00:27:55.580 --> 00:27:58.600
So we know that these
are early days of cloud.

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:01.690
But what we want to make sure
is that customers understand

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:05.565
that they don't need to shy
away from adopting cloud

00:28:05.565 --> 00:28:06.940
because they think
they can't get

00:28:06.940 --> 00:28:09.040
the performance or the
semantics that they're

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:13.270
expecting from their
applications in the cloud.

00:28:13.270 --> 00:28:16.360
If you're needing horizontal
scale and relational

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:19.060
semantics for
example, you know you

00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:22.030
don't have to worry
about not being

00:28:22.030 --> 00:28:24.660
able to do that in the cloud,
because, for example, we

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:26.530
have Cloud Spanner
on Google Cloud that

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:30.050
will allow you to have
that without compromises.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:33.940
So we expect most of
the cloud migration

00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:37.950
to have in the next five-ish
years, three to five years,

00:28:37.950 --> 00:28:38.451
and beyond.

00:28:38.451 --> 00:28:40.949
There are many reasons for not
being in the cloud right now.

00:28:40.949 --> 00:28:42.910
As I said, you've
already made commitments.

00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:44.368
You've already
bought the machines.

00:28:44.368 --> 00:28:46.240
You've already got
data center space.

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:49.930
You've already got co-lo space
for your data infrastructure.

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:54.310
So it will take time, but I
know that a lot of companies

00:28:54.310 --> 00:28:56.350
are looking at
migrating to the cloud.

00:28:56.350 --> 00:28:59.310
And it's important that they
see the full picture and all

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:01.060
the options available
to them so that they

00:29:01.060 --> 00:29:03.580
don't have to shy away from
it for let's say performance

00:29:03.580 --> 00:29:07.240
or manageability or
any of those reasons.

00:29:07.240 --> 00:29:11.380
So moving on, why not just
use a non-relational database

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:13.610
on prem or in the cloud?

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:15.880
So I think I touched
upon that earlier.

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:19.630
You know, if you want to use
a non-relational database,

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.530
that's totally up to you.

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:23.980
However, non-relational
databases

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:26.110
come with the complexity
that we talked about.

00:29:26.110 --> 00:29:27.520
Right?

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:29.250
If you need
relational semantics,

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:32.650
and most mission critical
data does, like bank systems,

00:29:32.650 --> 00:29:35.854
telco systems,
health care systems,

00:29:35.854 --> 00:29:38.020
when you need relational
semantics and you're trying

00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:39.492
to use a non-relational
database,

00:29:39.492 --> 00:29:41.950
what that means is you've given
up on transactional system,

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:43.630
you've given up on
strong consistency,

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:47.200
you've given on structured
data, you've given up on SQL--

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:48.790
SQL lite is not
the same as equal.

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:51.160
Anybody who's used
a SQL lite system

00:29:51.160 --> 00:29:53.020
can know that it's not SQL.

00:29:53.020 --> 00:29:55.090
So you've given
up on all of those

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:57.850
to get the scale with these
non-relational databases.

00:29:57.850 --> 00:29:59.960
And if that works
for you, great.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:03.481
But it does mean that
if you need those things

00:30:03.481 --> 00:30:05.230
you have to build them
in the application.

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:07.146
And anybody who's even
attempted to build them

00:30:07.146 --> 00:30:09.970
knows that this is
super complicated code.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:13.990
It's got so many
edge cases that you

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:17.320
have to think about
and take care of.

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:19.900
It's brittle, because
actually building--

00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:22.240
rolling your own transaction
in the application layer

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:27.300
is hard, or in the sharded
solution, for example,

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:30.710
doing the logic to
join between two shards

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:33.730
and all those kinds of
things are really hard.

00:30:33.730 --> 00:30:35.980
And again, if you like doing
that kind of stuff that's

00:30:35.980 --> 00:30:37.540
great, but for most
people, they're

00:30:37.540 --> 00:30:39.050
trying to just build
their applications

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:40.750
to run their businesses
and they don't want

00:30:40.750 --> 00:30:41.916
to worry about these things.

00:30:41.916 --> 00:30:45.610
In that case,
non-relational databases

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:49.730
just don't cut it
for what they need.

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:50.230
OK.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:52.660
What's the difference between
Cloud Spanner and BigQuery?

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:55.570
Can't I just use BigQuery?

00:30:55.570 --> 00:31:00.760
So it's basically the difference
between OLTP systems and OLAP

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:02.175
systems.

00:31:02.175 --> 00:31:04.300
So are you looking for an
enterprise data warehouse

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:06.730
or are you looking for
a operational mission

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:09.095
critical database?

00:31:09.095 --> 00:31:11.470
That is literally when you
would choose one or the other,

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:13.540
with Cloud Spanner
for OLTP systems

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:20.380
and BigQuery for OLAP systems
or enterprise data warehouse.

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:25.510
So if I were to think about
it in the on prem world

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:27.580
or in the traditional
world, it would

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:30.280
be the difference between
Oracle or SQL Server

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:33.820
and [INAUDIBLE] or Teradata.

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:35.440
That's the real difference.

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:36.910
And in fact we're
basically trying

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:40.960
to make it simple for customers
to use our operational data

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:43.750
in a data warehouse with
Cloud Spanner or BigQuery.

00:31:43.750 --> 00:31:47.770
So you don't have to like
have complicated tools

00:31:47.770 --> 00:31:51.586
for ETLing data out of
your relational database,

00:31:51.586 --> 00:31:52.960
out of your
operational database,

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:54.754
and moving into
your data warehouse.

00:31:54.754 --> 00:31:56.170
There are simple
tools that we are

00:31:56.170 --> 00:31:59.440
working on that will basically
allow you to quickly transfer

00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:02.019
data from Cloud Spanner into
BigQuery and then over time

00:32:02.019 --> 00:32:04.060
that you don't even have
to do the detail at all.

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:07.570
It can be as simple
as a button thing.

00:32:07.570 --> 00:32:09.750
Moving on, does Cloud
Spanner have every feature

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:12.630
that traditional databases do?

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:13.870
No.

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:16.440
It's a different
take on the system.

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:19.565
So there are specific
sort of features

00:32:19.565 --> 00:32:21.940
that Cloud Spanner doesn't
have now that we're working on

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:25.500
to fill those gaps,
like DML obviously,

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:31.120
and backups and things like
that, and triggers and queues.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:33.480
These are all part of
our roadmap, right?

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:37.590
So I would hope that our
audience and our customers

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:43.560
give us some time, because
we just launched last year

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:46.920
and traditional databases have
been around for 30, 40 years.

00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:49.950
So we're trying and
moving very fast.

00:32:49.950 --> 00:32:53.310
And we're going to close those
relational database feature

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:55.060
gaps that are critical, right?

00:32:55.060 --> 00:32:56.970
There are certain
things that are not

00:32:56.970 --> 00:32:59.760
critical for relational
databases that somehow

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:03.660
are supposed to be, you know
stored procedures for example.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:06.300
That's just a way of pushing
down application logic

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:10.710
into the database layer,
which we don't actually

00:33:10.710 --> 00:33:15.810
think that's the way we
want to go with our system.

00:33:15.810 --> 00:33:20.730
We wanted to have a lean mean
database that can do the things

00:33:20.730 --> 00:33:23.430
you need it to do, which
is strong consistency,

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.080
traditional--

00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:27.370
sorry, transactions, et cetera.

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:30.390
And we want to give this to
you in global scale, right?

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:33.270
But we think that application
developers know best

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:35.760
how to write their
applications and that's

00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:38.030
the way we want to
offer this service.

00:33:38.030 --> 00:33:40.680
So with Cloud Spanner, with
a new take on databases,

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:45.480
we were able to go back
to first principles

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:47.490
and think about
why databases exist

00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:50.100
and what they're needed
for, what they're used for.

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:56.370
And we were able to cut out
all the sort of flack that

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:59.040
has cruft that has been building
up in databases for a while.

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:02.840
And we were able to go back to
first principle for databases

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:08.790
and then provide the features
and functionality that

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:11.580
are truly core for
databases, especially

00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:12.629
relational databases.

00:34:12.629 --> 00:34:14.670
And as I said, yes, it
doesn't have every feature

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:16.199
right now which we're going
to [INAUDIBLE] but it also

00:34:16.199 --> 00:34:18.449
has features that no
other relational database,

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:20.010
or in fact no
other database has,

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:22.530
which is global, strong
consistency, replication out

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:27.395
of the box, and transparent
securities built in

00:34:27.395 --> 00:34:30.639
and transparent et cetera.

00:34:30.639 --> 00:34:36.960
Next question, why is sharding
a database so difficult?

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:43.800
So if you ever had
to shard a database,

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:47.040
this would be an obvious
question or obvious answer

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:48.780
really.

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:51.510
So think about a database
with a bunch of tables, right?

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:53.676
And now think about the
fact that you can't actually

00:34:53.676 --> 00:34:57.290
buy a big enough box to hold all
the data in all those tables.

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:59.320
So what you have to do then is--

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:00.961
and I'm going to simplify here.

00:35:00.961 --> 00:35:03.210
There are many things that
go into consideration here.

00:35:03.210 --> 00:35:05.190
But at the simplest
level, what you

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:07.740
have to do is basically
take the table--

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:09.930
if you have 10 tables,
take each table

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:11.910
and then put it in a
different database,

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:14.910
because that way at least
the dataset for that table

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:19.830
is going to fit in your
traditional single box machine.

00:35:19.830 --> 00:35:23.650
So you have 10 tables across
10 different databases.

00:35:23.650 --> 00:35:25.500
So you know it's, of
course you can just

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:29.280
keep using your tooling and
you can keep using transactions

00:35:29.280 --> 00:35:32.300
all for within that table or
maybe two tables with a table

00:35:32.300 --> 00:35:33.900
and index let's say.

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:38.010
But it behaves exactly the same
as one database for that one

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:39.720
table and its one index.

00:35:39.720 --> 00:35:43.170
But if you ever have to do
anything across those tables,

00:35:43.170 --> 00:35:46.230
then you have to write
the transaction logic

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:49.260
in the application
to do the join

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:51.780
and to do the
transactions on them.

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:55.830
So you essentially will
have to write the protocol

00:35:55.830 --> 00:35:57.600
to commit a transaction
that is done

00:35:57.600 --> 00:35:59.850
in a database inside
the application layer

00:35:59.850 --> 00:36:04.830
without having the engine
that runs the system.

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:07.620
The database engine is what runs
these transactions, et cetera,

00:36:07.620 --> 00:36:10.230
and does the consistency
and guarantees, et cetera.

00:36:10.230 --> 00:36:14.490
So to do that in the application
layer is extremely hard

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:19.001
and, as I said, very brittle,
because what happens when one--

00:36:19.001 --> 00:36:21.000
if you're doing a two
phase commit, for example,

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.670
what happens if one
shard is unavailable

00:36:23.670 --> 00:36:24.840
but the other are committed?

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:27.360
So do you roll back that
shard or do you retrial

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:29.620
on the first shard, right?

00:36:29.620 --> 00:36:32.790
And what happens if
during the commit

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:34.810
there was a network outage?

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:37.950
So do you know whether the
transaction committed or not?

00:36:37.950 --> 00:36:40.410
Like how do you recover from
that state, et cetera, right?

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:41.880
So there's many,
many things that

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:51.930
go into making shard database
work well and all of them

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:57.630
basically make the application
developer's life very hard.

00:36:57.630 --> 00:37:01.830
So those are some of
the questions we had.

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:04.560
I don't think we have
any more questions,

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:07.920
so I think we'll call it a day.

00:37:07.920 --> 00:37:12.150
And thank you again for
watching and for tuning

